I chunk geometry: quantum 16 (attn block 16, state chunk 1), checkpoint interval 2048, max_tok 16 -> prefill chunks of at most 16
I prefix resume: exact -- a hit resumes only where an idle server's cold prefill of the same prompt starts a chunk (every 16 tokens) and a prompt's tail snapshot is taken where its last chunk starts, so the hit recomputes the cold run's own chunks and answers bit-identically; a turn re-prefills up to one chunk more than --prefix-resume nearest
I checkpoint slots: a byte budget: 45 linear-state + sliding-window snapshot(s) of 22.59 MiB in pinned host memory (1016.72 MiB of the 1024 MiB asked), none of it on the card; that is 6.4 sessions of the 7 snapshots geometric-backoff keeps for a 32768-token one at a 2048-token interval, its running turn's included
I kv tiers: off (no --prefix-cache-mib); a finished conversation's KV stays in VRAM until the cache evicts it
I prefix cache: 4 paged group(s) in 2-token entries; checkpoints every 2048 tokens (45 slots of 5.65 MiB a rank, pinned host memory), retention 'geometric-backoff'
I end-of-request snapshots: on (RADIANCE_EXPERIMENTAL_PREFIX_EORS) -- a decode step whose row completes a position on the 16-token resume grid past its prompt snapshots the state there, a prompt ending on the grid snapshots its end and every prompt the start of its last chunk, so a conversation's next turn resumes at most 15 tokens before the reply's last computed position and recomputes the cold run's own chunks from there; such a step is not issued ahead, and the trim after a publish keeps the entries of the chain just published that the request recomputed rather than adopted
I prefix cache: a trunk layer's windowed group is not prefix-cached -- a hit resumes from a window snapshot (a checkpoint slot)
